There is a factory website that has several machines each running the same number of processes. Write a solution to find the average time each machine takes to complete a process.
The time to complete a process is the 'end' timestamp minus the 'start' timestamp. The average time is calculated by the total time to complete every process on the machine divided by the number of processes that were run.
The resulting table should have the machine_id along with the average time as processing_time, which should be rounded to 3 decimal places.
Return the result table in any order.

My solution :

-- Global view --

SELECT *
FROM Activity

-- View of one clean table --

SELECT A.machine_id, A.process_id, A.activity_type as Act_start, B.activity_type as Act_end, A.timestamp as time_start, B.timestamp as time_end
FROM Activity A, Activity B
WHERE A.activity_type = 'start' AND B.activity_type = 'end' AND A.process_id = B.process_id AND A.machine_id = B.machine_id


-- Duration -- 

SELECT A.machine_id, A.process_id, A.activity_type as Act_start, B.activity_type as Act_end, A.timestamp as time_start, B.timestamp as time_end,
B.timestamp - A.timestamp as duration 
FROM Activity A, Activity B
WHERE A.activity_type = 'start' AND B.activity_type = 'end' AND A.process_id = B.process_id AND A.machine_id = B.machine_id

-- Processing_time --

SELECT machine_id, ROUND(AVG(duration),3) as processing_time
FROM (SELECT A.machine_id, A.process_id, A.activity_type as Act_start, B.activity_type as Act_end, A.timestamp as time_start, B.timestamp as time_end,
B.timestamp - A.timestamp as duration 
FROM Activity A, Activity B
WHERE A.activity_type = 'start' AND B.activity_type = 'end' AND A.process_id = B.process_id AND A.machine_id = B.machine_id
) t
GROUP BY machine_id

